{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_workflow_entrypoints.py::test_mcp_run_workflow_is_async_and_pollable` polls `get_workflow_status` 50 times with `time.sleep(0.1)` between polls (lines 134-143). That gives the background `run_workflow` a fixed 5-second wall-clock window to settle, and the test then fails with \"background run never settled\" (line 144).\n\nMeasured on a macOS dev host, 2026-11-04 PST:\n- It failed once in a 4-worker targeted run while `make lint` and `make typecheck` were also running.\n- It failed once more in isolation, minutes later, with other sessions active on the host.\n- It then passed 26 of 26 runs on unmodified `origin/main` (`e0bfa4e5154`), and 30 of 30 further runs with the synonymous-destined-cricket change. 16 runs of each arm were 4-way concurrent.\n\nSo the failure tracks host load, not a code difference. A fixed wall-clock deadline on a background thread's completion is the class of flake AGENTS.md says to root-cause rather than retry.\n\nDiscovered while verifying synonymous-destined-cricket (40c3-d22a-3872-4c8c).\n\n## Testing\n\n`pytest tests/interfaces/store/test_workflow_entrypoints.py -k mcp_run_workflow` passes normally, passes with the background run delayed 6 s, fails when the run never settles, and passes 20 of 20 runs at 4-way concurrency.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The test waits on the run's own completion signal or a state change, not a fixed 5-second wall clock, and still fails when the run never settles.\n- [x] The test passes 20 of 20 runs under 4-way concurrent load."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794510628984101001,"uuid":"8d084a11-d759-4243-8c26-23ef98cb8ae6"}